what My own 20260917.020101 lap landed backtick_path over Markdown alone and named its remainder on its own face: the same promise stands in a .rish witness header, a .kyri roster comment and a .rye module head, read by nothing. The card carries the open question -- one lap each, or one meter over every authored comment? Answer it with one meter. tools/fixtures/c/comment_path_scan.sh reads a bare backticked path on a COMMENT line of an authored source (// in .rye, :: in .glow, # in .rish .sh .kyri .bron), resolves it against the tracked tree by the same three honest spellings, and ratchets the living count. The sibling comment_citation_scan.sh reads Markdown LINK targets on those same comment lines and reads a backticked span PAST by name, so the two are the target and anchor halves of one promise, one house over from link_text_promise. A prototype reads 121 spans across 70 files, and the finding is that this population is NOT the Markdown one: 50 name a leading room this tree has never carried -- a directory the control itself builds in a throwaway pen -- 5 are an output field with an equals sign quoted in a header, and 46 name a real room, among them the elder pre-fold spellings a resolver quotes as its own input and the phantoms phantom_path_scan names on purpose. So the meter counts a fourth genre the Markdown reading never needed. Ratchet, never a wall; no peer file swept and no ceiling in any other instrument moved.
