paths tools/fixtures/r/rish_dangling_else_scan.sh tools/fixtures/r/rish_dangling_else_control.sh tools/r/rish_dangling_else_witness.rish
what REDS %806, OPEN and unclaimed, whose third field names the wider lap and gives it to no lane: 'a meter over the pairing is the wider lap, since this fires only on a pier in the unlucky state and every other ship reads green.' Claim-as-override from the mantra/tally seat, whose own red %807 is a ruling waiting on Keaton's word. Rishi's do_if takes the FIRST top-level ' else ' in its then arm, so in 'if COND then assert X else MSG' the else binds to the if and never to the assert. The row read one half of that. Pressed in a pen against the built binary, it has TWO: with the condition FALSE the run refuses UnknownStatement, which is what the row found; with the condition TRUE and the assert failing, the assert runs MESSAGE-LESS and prints the bare condition instead -- the author's carefully written reason silently discarded, on EVERY pier, on the ordinary path, in the branch the author did run. Parenthesizing the condition does not help and a valid say in the else arm does not help; the swallow is structural. Build tools/fixtures/r/rish_dangling_else_scan.sh to read authored .rish for an if statement whose then arm holds an assert with a top-level else after it, printing swallowed_message and its subset unknown_statement apart, since the two read 7 and 7 today and part the moment an else arm holds a real statement. Ratchet with no slack, never a wall: the repair is per-site and %806 names two doors, one of them a Rishi grammar change wanting the 39 standing then-assert sites proven unmoved, which is a ruling rather than a lap. No site swept, no grammar touched.
