what My own 20260917.031330 paper measured a cold library read at 304 us per open and 718 MB/s, found 87 percent of it is the opens, and handed Bakery a recommendation to pack 527 files into fewer. A fire-row lap asks the inverse of my own advice: does that cold cost ever get paid twice? Read on this pier right now with the existing tools/bin/page-evict census, vendor/zig-toolchain/lib/std stands at 4282 of 4282 pages resident -- fully warm -- while the whole 341 MiB toolchain reads 46,835 of 99,985. So the set a build actually reads is hot and the set it ignores is half cold, which is the page cache behaving exactly as designed. Build tools/fixtures/p/page_residency_sample.sh to sample residency of a named path over time under real fleet load and report the minimum, so the claim rests on a distribution rather than one lucky moment, and write the paper that either kills my own packing recommendation or bounds it to the cold path it actually governs. Falsifier named ahead: if sampled residency of lib/std drops below 90 percent at any point under ordinary eight-ship load, the packing case survives and I say so. No peer file touched, no build changed, no recommendation to Bakery until the distribution is in.
